//? Title: Computus: Integer Cycles and the Gregorian Easter Rule
//? Subtitle: Actual arithmetic steps, exceptional week corrections and the original 2000–2040 example
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a year, a calendar program needs the month and day of Gregorian Easter.
The answer follows ecclesiastical lunar tables, a fixed 21 March equinox and
the following-Sunday rule, rather than observations of the astronomical Moon.
The Anonymous Gregorian integer algorithm combines a nineteen-year lunar index,
century corrections and weekday arithmetic. We expose its actual native steps,
explain the two exceptional week corrections, and retain the original notable
years and 2000–2040 chart. A complete 5.7-million-year rule period supplies exact
date frequencies. Positive int years are supported as Gregorian rule projections,
with bounded range summaries and explicit treatment of early proleptic dates.
)

\toc

> A hand-checkable year: 2024

Gregorian Easter is the Sunday strictly following the ecclesiastical paschal
full moon on or after the fixed ecclesiastical equinox, 21 March. Equality of
the moon date with a Sunday therefore leads to a later Sunday. This rule uses
tables; it is not an astronomical full-moon/equinox calculation. The US Naval
Observatory distinguishes these definitions and their occasional different dates.

For positive year Y, let a=Y mod 19. This is an index from zero through eighteen;
the traditional golden number is a+1. Split the year into complete centuries
b=floor(Y/100) and year within century c=Y mod 100. The remaining integer steps
are the actual quantities returned by computus_gregorian_trace():

\[
\begin{aligned}
d&=\lfloor b/4\rfloor,& e&=b\bmod4,& f&=\lfloor(b+8)/25\rfloor,&g&=\lfloor(b-f+1)/3\rfloor,\\
h&=(19a+b-d-g+15)\bmod30,&i&=\lfloor c/4\rfloor,&k&=c\bmod4,\\
l&=(32+2e+2i-h-k)\bmod7,&m&=\lfloor(a+11h+22l)/451\rfloor,\\
q&=h+l-7m+114,&M&=\lfloor q/31\rfloor,&D&=(q\bmod31)+1.
\end{aligned}
\]
\alttext(Century quotients and remainders give d, e, f and g. The nominal lunar remainder h is modulo thirty; i and k split the year within century by four. The weekday remainder l is modulo seven. The special correction m divides a plus eleven h plus twenty-two l by 451. Encoding q gives the month by division by thirty-one and the day by its remainder plus one.)

All numerators and remainders are nonnegative for the supported positive years,
so C++ integer division agrees with these floors. For 2024, a=10 means golden
number 11; b=20, c=24, h=4 and l=5. With m=0, q=123 gives month three and day
31. The trace and method diagram below use the executing native calculation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/computus_steps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::computus_gregorian_trace(2024);
  if(t.date.month!=3 || t.date.day!=31 || t.a!=10 || t.h!=4 || t.l!=5 || t.m!=0)return 1;
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string_view,int>> quantities{{"a: Metonic index",t.a},
    {"golden number: a+1",t.a+1},{"b: complete centuries",t.b},{"c: year within century",t.c},
    {"d",t.d},{"e",t.e},{"f",t.f},{"g",t.g},{"h: nominal lunar remainder",t.h},
    {"i",t.i},{"k",t.k},{"l: weekday remainder",t.l},{"m: week correction",t.m},
    {"q: month/day encoding",t.encoding},{"fixed March coordinate",t.march_day}};
  std::print("| actual quantity | integer value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& [name,value]:quantities)std::print("| {} | {} |\n",name,value);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=4.4;box.height=2.1;box.font_size=18;
  d.node("year",{0,0},std::format("Gregorian rule year {}\na = {}; golden number {}\nb = {}; c = {}",t.year,t.a,t.a+1,t.b,t.c),box);
  box.fill={255,237,213};
  d.node("cycles",{6,0},std::format("Actual remainders\nh = {}; l = {}\nm = {}",t.h,t.l,t.m),box);
  box.fill={220,252,231};
  d.node("date",{12,0},std::format("Actual q = {}\nmonth {}; day {}\nMarch coordinate {}",t.encoding,t.date.month,t.date.day,t.march_day),box)
   .edge("year","cycles","cycles")
   .edge("cycles","date","decode q")
   .text({6,2.3},"One executing calculation: 2024 gives 31 March",23)
   .text({6,-2.3},"a is an index; the golden number is a+1",21)
   .save_svg("build/computus_steps.svg");
}
```
