\alttext(The 41 yearly Easter coordinates jump between March and April on a fixed March axis. The earliest point is March 23 in 2008; the latest is April 25 in 2038.)

> A period of the arithmetic rule, and its date frequencies

Nineteen years repeat a, not the complete date: 2000 gives April 23 and 2019
April 21. A sufficient exact period for the full rule is L=5,700,000 years.
This is a period argument, without a claim that the following derivation proves
minimality. Under Y→Y+L, a and c are unchanged, while

\[
\Delta b=57000,\quad\Delta d=14250,\quad\Delta f=2280,\quad\Delta g=18240.
\]
\alttext(Shifting by 5,700,000 years adds 57,000 centuries, 14,250 quarter-century groups, 2,280 units to f and 18,240 to g.)

The shift in h's unreduced numerator is 57000−14250−18240=24510=817×30, so h
is unchanged. Since the century shift is divisible by four, e is unchanged;
i and k depend only on c. Therefore l,m and the decoded date repeat as well.
The argument is an identity of the rule, not a prediction of lunar motion.

The next program scans one complete period, years 1700 through 5,701,699,
with an explicit cap of 5,700,000. No per-year dataset or large array is stored:
the native summary retains 35 exact integer bins and first-tie extrema. The
percentages are proportions of this deterministic rule period. They describe
that period's nonuniform dates, rather than a random probability distribution
or historical observance frequencies.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/computus_cycle.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto s=dm::algorithms::computus_gregorian_summary(1700,5701699,5700000);
  if(s.year_count!=5700000 || std::accumulate(s.date_counts.begin(),s.date_counts.end(),std::uint64_t{0})!=s.year_count || s.spread_days!=34)return 1;
  std::vector<double> days,percent;
  std::print("| month/day | exact years in rule period | percent of rule years |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t bin=0;bin<s.date_counts.size();++bin) {
    const int coordinate=22+static_cast<int>(bin),month=coordinate<=31?3:4,day=coordinate<=31?coordinate:coordinate-31;
    const double share=100.*s.date_counts[bin]/s.year_count;
    days.push_back(coordinate);percent.push_back(share);
    std::print("| {}/{} | {} | {:.6f} |\n",month,day,s.date_counts[bin],share);
  }
  std::print("\nRule years {}; actual week corrections {}.\nFirst earliest year {}, date {}/{}; first latest year {}, date {}/{}.\n",s.year_count,s.corrected_years,s.earliest_year,s.earliest.month,s.earliest.day,s.latest_year,s.latest.month,s.latest.day);
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::computus_gregorian_trace(2000),b=dm::algorithms::computus_gregorian_trace(2019);
  if(a.a!=b.a || a.date.day==b.date.day)return 1;
  std::print("Nineteen-year control: same a={}, but dates {}/{} and {}/{}.\n",a.a,a.date.month,a.date.day,b.date.month,b.date.day);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.bars(days,percent,"",{37,99,235},.8)
      .title("One complete 5,700,000-year Gregorian rule period")
      .x_label("fixed March coordinate; 32 = April 1").y_label("percent of projected rule years")
      .x_limits(21,57).y_limits(0,4.5).size(950,580)
      .save_svg("build/computus_cycle.svg");
}
```
