> Conclusion

The original notable dates and 2000–2040 extrema are preserved. Actual integer
records distinguish the Metonic index from the golden number and expose why
the two exceptional Sunday cases subtract a week. The plot uses a consistent
March coordinate, rather than a mislabeled day-of-year. A derived 5.7-million-year
period and exact native frequencies describe the rule's long cycle, while the
positive-year domain and bounded summaries make its computational limits clear.

> References

- Anonymous (1876), [To Find Easter](https://doi.org/10.1038/013487a0), Nature 13, 487. Historical Gregorian arithmetic rule; the modern m-form used by the library is stated explicitly in this report.
- U.S. Naval Observatory, [The Date of Easter](https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/easter). Ecclesiastical definitions, the 1954 example and an independent Oudin algorithm used in development checks.
- U.S. Census Bureau, [Easter Dates from 1600 to 2099](https://www.census.gov/data/software/x13as/genhol/easter-dates.html). Published date controls for the original and exceptional examples; the executable report calculates its own rule dates.
